8 #edb/ref 17592186370582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064380 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064380 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064380 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064380 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064380 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "72b11864-0846-5b22-aa45-5a20079afafb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186370025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186370591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186506596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Related Rate With a Sphere: Word Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064381 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Related Rates With Circles and Spheres" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1050 1260.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186064393 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bdb82b74-3fdc-5083-a106-20c7dc1287f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065317 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065317 #edb/ref 1061 906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065317 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f58c76e-8d14-575a-b5de-62cc2003eb38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065317 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065318 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065318 #edb/ref 1061 907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a12978db-32b6-52fc-a426-abfda5e434ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065319 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065319 #edb/ref 1061 910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065319 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c671f5f-a9e8-5694-8457-ab3f9df8b239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065319 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065320 #edb/ref 1061 913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065320 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "318a88e0-e4a9-5b31-a8c3-9cb7712ff53b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065320 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065384 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we're told that the radius of a circle is **growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second**, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27d0543834333202411a9700401b7e33329a255c7103c9f2ebe394d8c1cf694b.png)\n\nIf the radius of the circle is growing, then its area is also increasing. How do we calculate the **rate at which the area is increasing**?\n\nThe rate at which the area is changing can be expressed using a derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is the area of the circle, measured in $\\textrm{cm}^2,$ and $t$ is the time in seconds. \n\nWe know that the area is related to the radius $r$ as\n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the radius of a circle is growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt} = 2 \\, \\textrm{cm}.$ Substituting this information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot 2\\pi r=4\\pi r\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd, that's it, we're done! \n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $r$ at that moment into the above. So, for example, if we want to compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at the moment when $r=6\\,\\textrm{cm},$ we plug this into the above and get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4\\pi \\cdot 6 = 24\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065384 #edb/ref 1065 488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065384 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6925e269-127a-5cf1-8116-aa809d1cf42f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065384 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296561 #edb/ref 1080 "8\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296561 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296561 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f48b03f4-de1f-5518-8d7b-f0622fb8986b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296734 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186296561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296734 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296734 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296734 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186296561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296734 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3697c49e-740a-5aaa-a707-4701abf7681d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297434 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186296734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297434 #edb/ref 1069 "q-315860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297434 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297434 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e8/e850cd14f3b4f791c79c136d0fc799fd17fdc23eeab37e67ad2811f3ff7b6d0e.png)\n\nA snowball is melting in the sun and slowly shrinking. Determine the rate at which the surface area of the snowball is decreasing, given that the radius is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s}$ and its surface area at that moment is $100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb5b4de1-d892-5870-99f2-1c26e26d0697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186297434 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316482 #edb/ref 1080 "20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316482 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316482 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f4804743-754e-52a4-ac46-58a24afecd98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316860 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316860 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316860 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316860 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316860 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "87f30560-b7a8-5fe7-a866-713cb0a11c66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317539 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317539 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/28/287add661cff085d1090295f67f87ee80eeaa3d8301ee6632ab54a812a6cd119.png)\n\nA spherical hailstone is melting uniformly, causing its radius to change at a rate of $\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.$ If the radius at a certain moment is $15\\,\\text{mm},$ what is the rate at which the surface area is decreasing?\n\nThe surface area of the hailstone is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6cc0140-a8eb-5088-99b0-8b7a894e4ee5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317539 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=15\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the surface area formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }$ and $r=15.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (15) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the hailstone is decreasing at a rate of $20{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" #edb/r